---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const dashCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Start from the decision', note: '"What will someone DO differently based on this screen?" If no answer exists, it is decoration. Dashboards exist to change decisions — everything else is wallpaper.' },
    { math: '2 · The 5-second rule', note: 'A viewer should grasp the state of things in five seconds: headline states the takeaway, one chart proves it, detail sits below the fold. Pyramid principle (Barbara Minto): conclusion first, support after.' },
    { math: '3 · Context, not just numbers', note: 'Every metric needs a comparison — vs last week, vs target, vs segment. A "1,247" floating alone is trivia. "1,247 vs 1,100 target, down 3% WoW" is information.' },
    { math: '4 · Design for the 3 a.m. check', note: 'No zooming, no hovering requirements, no 14 decimals. Mobile-readable, colour-blind safe, load instant. The best dashboard is the one people actually open.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/advanced/dashboards-and-data-storytelling">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Dashboards and data storytelling are <strong>where analysis earns its keep — or
    doesn't</strong>. The craft: lead with the conclusion, prove it with one clear chart, provide
    drill-down for the sceptics, and design for how people actually consume data (quickly,
    distractedly, on phones).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four rules of dashboards people actually use" widget="equationStep" config={dashCfg} />

  <h2>Storytelling structure that works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Situation → Complication → Resolution</strong> (Minto's SCQA): "Sales grew all year (S); but the growth is concentrated in one segment (C); here's where to invest next (R)". The frame turns charts into an argument.</li>
    <li><strong>One chart, one message:</strong> if a chart needs two paragraphs of explanation, split it or simplify it. Animation and interactivity serve exploration — the first read should be static and instant.</li>
    <li><strong>Know the audience's vocabulary:</strong> executives need decisions and risks; analysts need methods and caveats. Same data, different first sentence.</li>
    <li><strong>Report uncertainty in plain words:</strong> "early signal, needs another month of data" beats false precision. Trust is the compounding asset of every analyst's career.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Minto, The Pyramid Principle; Knaflic, Storytelling with Data; Few, Information Dashboard Design.</p>
</TopicPage>
